\description{
Frequencies of 16 lexico-grammatical features in 480 texts from six
registers of the web, as used in multi-dimensional analysis (Biber, 1988).
Its parameters are based on the web register data of Ehret and Taboada
(2021a, 2021b). Three dimensions underlie the features, and one column is
the sum of three others. For exploratory factor analysis and, on the dimension
scores, cluster analysis.
}
\details{
True model: each text has scores on three dimensions, involved
(vs informational) production, narrative and informational elaboration.
They are the register mean plus normal noise with SD 0.6 (involved,
elaborated) or 0.9 (narrative):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{register              texts  involved  narrative  elaborated
forum discussion         90       1.3        0.0        -1.0
personal blog            90       1.1        0.5        -0.9
news report             110      -0.2        0.5         0.3
encyclopedia article     60      -1.1        0.0         0.5
research article         70      -1.0       -0.5         1.8
recipe                   60       0.0       -1.0        -1.6
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Each feature has a latent score: the dimension scores times its
loadings, plus normal noise with variance 1 minus the sum of its squared
loadings. Loadings are 0.75 (\code{contractions}, \code{first_person}), 0.7
(\code{second_person}), 0.6 (\code{negation}), 0.55 (\code{adverbs}), 0.5
(\code{private_verbs}), -0.55 (\code{prepositions}) and -0.8 (\code{nouns}) on the
involved dimension; 0.85 (\code{past_tense}), 0.55 (\code{third_person}) and 0.5
(\code{perfect_aspect}) on the narrative dimension; 0.75 (\code{nominalisations}),
0.5 (\code{attributive_adjectives}) and 0.45 (\code{conjuncts}) on informational
elaboration. \code{present_tense} loads 0.7 on involved and -0.4 on
narrative; \code{word_length} loads -0.45 on involved and 0.7 on
elaboration. The rate per 1,000 words is
\code{base_rate * exp(spread * latent)}, with the base rates and spreads in
\code{data-raw/web_registers.R}, and the number of occurrences in a text is
Poisson with mean \code{rate * words / 1000}; the columns give occurrences
per 1,000 words. \code{word_length} is \code{4.6 + 0.32 * latent}. Text length is
log-normal with a median of 900 words, limited to 400 to 4,000. Because
of the log scale and the Poisson counts, a factor analysis of the rates
gives smaller loadings than these; log rates come closer. Since
\code{pronouns} is the sum of three other columns, the correlation matrix of
all features is singular.

The base rates and spreads were chosen so that the register means
approach those of the same six registers in the data of Ehret and
Taboada (2021b), whose texts come from the Corpus of Online Registers of
English (Egbert et al., 2015). Most simulated register means are within
25\% of theirs. The largest differences are in pronouns and contractions,
most of all in recipes, which in their data use more first and second
person pronouns than three dimensions can reproduce. The differences
between registers on the dimensions are larger than in their data, so
that the registers separate more clearly.
}
